WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.920
Leadership communication should explain PQC risk without hype.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.960
The message is not that every encrypted record is suddenly exposed or that a single date

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.040
will change everything.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.080
The better message is that some widely used public-key assumptions may not remain adequate

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.180
in the presence of cryptographically relevant quantum computers,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.930
and that migration has enough dependency work that responsible organizations should

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.740
prepare before urgency forces rushed decisions.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.095
Governance turns that message into action.

00:00:25.095 --> 00:00:28.345
Policies may need to describe approved algorithms, library expectations,

00:00:28.345 --> 00:00:32.135
certificate practices, data retention considerations, vendor requirements,

00:00:32.135 --> 00:00:34.862
exception handling, and documentation standards.

00:00:34.862 --> 00:00:38.560
Procurement teams may need questions for cloud providers, appliance vendors,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.090
identity platforms, managed service providers, and software suppliers.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.570
Risk registers should track systems with long-lived confidential data,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.755
unsupported dependencies, migration blockers, and accepted residual risk.

00:00:49.755 --> 00:00:52.320
Executives usually do not need deep cryptographic theory.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.810
They need practical timelines, inventory progress, decisions required, funding needs,

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.900
dependency risks, vendor readiness, and consequences of delay.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.400
A useful dashboard might show what percentage of exposed services have been inventoried,

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.580
which high-value systems depend on quantum-vulnerable public-key mechanisms,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.880
which vendors have committed support, and which pilots have produced usable results.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.775
Training matters because many teams touch cryptography without thinking of themselves as

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.530
cryptography teams.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:18.700
Developers choose libraries.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.435
Platform teams manage certificates.

00:01:20.435 --> 00:01:22.850
Operations teams troubleshoot handshakes.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.270
Procurement teams ask vendors about roadmap support.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.585
Security teams assess risk.

00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:29.595
Good governance gives each group enough shared language to coordinate.

00:01:29.595 --> 00:01:33.480
That shared language is what prevents PQC from becoming either panic or paperwork.
